@flexiarg creativity-commons/contributor-boundaries
@title Contributor Boundaries
@keywords boundaries, access, permissions, protocol, revocation, Ostrom 1A
@audience institution designers, agent operators, maintainers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 1A (user boundaries), as transposed in Corneli 2016, "An institutional approach to computational social creativity" (ICCC 2016), Table 2 and section "1A. User boundaries". Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29. Derived from the paper, not mined from runs.
@transposes institutions/user-boundaries
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@references [creativity-commons/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/place-boundaries]

! conclusion:
  The population of Producers who can add to or alter the resource is
  clearly defined, and the definition is recorded where a test can read
  it: on each modifiable object (who may change this) or on each
  Producer (what this one may change).

  + context:
    Humans and agents share repositories, trackers and message buses.
    "Anyone can edit" works only because violating the site's
    principles can lead to a block; the boundary is open at entry and
    enforced afterwards.

  + IF:
    A resource can be changed by more than one Producer, and some
    changes are costly to undo or to review.

  + HOWEVER:
    Tight boundaries at entry exclude useful contributions. Where a
    contribution is cheap to reject (a bug report), keep entry open and
    enforce later; tighten entry where review is costly (commit access,
    a patch mailed to maintainers).

  + THEN:
    Record who may change what. Before granting access, require that the
    Producer implements the Place's protocol (an API, callbacks, a
    message envelope, a signed commit). Revoke access for non-compliant
    behaviour after entry, and record the revocation.

  + BECAUSE:
    A test can only check a boundary that is written down. ACLs and
    protocol conformance are the computational form of Ostrom's
    "clear boundaries between legitimate users and nonusers".

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Example: agent commits carry Agent-Id and Dispatched-By trailers, so every change in a shared checkout names its Producer.]
    next[Example: in the FUTON Emacs pilot nothing left the fork; mailing GNU is a boundary crossing that needs Joe's sight first.]
